<TopicPage slug="subjects/welding/foundational/welding-safety-first">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first lesson in Welding, and every serious welding curriculum — the American
    Welding Society's SENSE programme included — opens in the same place: not with the
    machines or the molten steel, but with the hazards. That is not caution for its own sake.
    Welding is one of the few trades where the process itself radiates, fumes, spatters and
    shocks, all in the first minute of the first bead. You need no prior experience of
    metalwork, electricity or chemistry here — only everyday reasoning and a willingness to
    ask <em>why</em> a rule exists before memorising it.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the four hazard families of welding — arc radiation, fume, fire and electricity — and explain the mechanism behind each;</li>
    <li>order the defences against a hazard using the hierarchy of controls, and say why a ventilation duct outranks a respirator;</li>
    <li>select protective equipment for a given job and justify each item by the hazard it answers;</li>
    <li>handle compressed-gas cylinders by the physics of what is inside them;</li>
    <li>run the shop and site systems — hot-work permits, fire watches, lead inspections — as one connected system rather than a list of rules.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through the whole page: rules learned as rules are forgotten under
    pressure; mechanisms understood stay with you for a career. A welder who knows why the
    shade number matters can walk into a new shop, a new alloy or a new process and protect
    themselves correctly without being told.
  </p>

  <h2>A sun on earth</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the single most surprising fact in the trade. On a summer beach you would
    never lie in the sun without noticing the heat, yet a welder can stand next to an arc
    burning at roughly 6,000 °C — about as hot as the sun's surface — and feel only gentle
    warmth on their face. The arc delivers its damage through radiation the body cannot
    sense in real time: ultraviolet light, the same band that causes sunburn, arriving at
    intensities no beach produces. An unprotected glance of a second or two can leave the
    surface of the eye inflamed hours later.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="bet the arc's temperature" widget="estimate" config={wldArcEst} />
  <p>
    That gap between <em>cause</em> and <em>consequence</em> is the pattern behind every
    hazard on this page. The arc burns the eye hours after the glance; the fume builds its
    damage over years; the fire starts an hour after the last spark; the electric shock
    arrives with no warning at all. Welding safety is largely the craft of defending against
    injuries that announce themselves too late.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="match each hazard to its delay" widget="match" config={wldDelayMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> welding's hazards are real, present from the first arc, and
    delayed in their effects — which is why the trade answers them with systems, not
    reflexes. The next section names the system.
  </p>

  <h2>The hierarchy of controls</h2>
  <p>
    The professional frame is the <strong>hierarchy of controls</strong>, an ordering
    borrowed from industrial hygiene — the science of workplace health. It ranks defences by
    how much they depend on a person remembering to comply. First, <strong>eliminate or
    engineer</strong> the hazard away: remove the combustibles, fit local exhaust
    ventilation, put up welding screens. Second, <strong>administrative controls</strong>:
    procedures and training — permits, fire watches, daily equipment inspections. Last,
    <strong>personal protective equipment</strong> (PPE): helmet, gloves, leathers,
    respirator.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="the hierarchy of controls, top to bottom" widget="order" config={wldHierOrder} />
  <p>
    Beginners find this ordering backwards — they want to start with the helmet and the
    gloves, the gear they can see and touch. But the ordering is what makes welding safe as
    a career rather than safe once. A helmet protects one wearer during one arc; a
    ventilation system protects everyone in the building, every day, for years, including
    the day someone forgets their gear. PPE is never useless — it is simply the last line,
    not the first.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "safety means wearing the right PPE." It sounds
  reasonable because PPE is the visible face of safety — every poster shows the helmet and
  the gloves. But PPE sits at the bottom of the hierarchy precisely because it fails
  silently: a misplaced hood, a cracked filter, a respirator worn loosely all look like
  compliance while offering little protection. The correct picture: engineering controls
  first, procedures second, PPE as the final layer — each item answering a specific hazard
  the layers above could not fully remove.</p>

  <h2>Four hazards, four mechanisms</h2>
  <p>
    Everything a welder defends against falls into four families, and each family has a
    mechanism you can understand rather than a rule you must trust.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four hazards and the systems that tame them" widget="equationStep" config={hazardsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> each step pairs one hazard with the system that answers
    it — and the systems climb the hierarchy as you read. Steps one to four are the hazard
    families themselves; step five, the hot-work permit, is the administrative wrapper that
    holds them together on a real site. Keep this pairing in mind: every item of gear you
    meet later in the page exists because one of these four mechanisms exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Arc radiation: the burn without warmth</h2>
  <p>
    The arc emits intense ultraviolet and infrared radiation alongside its visible light.
    The ultraviolet component inflames the cornea — <strong>photokeratitis</strong>,
    known in the trade as "arc eye" or "welder's flash" — which strikes hours after
    exposure with the sensation of sand under the eyelids, and usually heals. The same
    radiation burns unprotected skin like sunburn, in minutes, and reflects off light
    surfaces so it can reach people who are not welding at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    The defence is filtration and coverage. Helmet filter plates are graded by
    <strong>shade number</strong> — the higher the number, the darker the filter. Stick and
    MIG welding at everyday amperages call for shades around 10–13; oxyacetylene gas
    welding, whose radiation is gentler, uses around 4–6. Full coverage of skin, plus
    welding screens around the work, protect everyone in sight of the arc — at ten metres
    an arc can still burn retinas and skin, which is why bystanders matter as much as
    welders.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="place the everyday filter plate" widget="place" config={wldShadePlace} />

  <h2>Fume: the slow hazard</h2>
  <p>
    Welding fume is not smoke. It is a fine aerosol of metal oxides and silicates, born
    when the arc vaporises metal and the vapour condenses into particles small enough to
    reach deep into the lungs. Its chemistry follows the work: mild steel carries iron and
    manganese (a neurotoxin under chronic exposure); galvanised steel adds zinc oxide,
    which causes metal fume fever — a flu-like illness arriving hours after the shift;
    stainless steel adds hexavalent chromium, a recognised carcinogen; paints and coatings
    add whatever was on the surface.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="match the metal to what its fume carries" widget="match" config={wldFumeMatch} />
  <p>
    Because the particles accumulate dose over years, the defences are engineered rather
    than heroic. Local exhaust ventilation — an extraction arm positioned close to the arc —
    captures the plume before it crosses the welder's breathing zone; respirators with
    fume-rated filters back it up where capture cannot reach. Exposure limits are measured
    in milligrams per cubic metre over an eight-hour shift, and the International Agency
    for Research on Cancer classifies welding fume overall as a carcinogen — the reason the
    trade now treats even mild-steel fume seriously.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> fume is just "smoke", and a brief cough is harmless. It
  seems reasonable because welding fume looks like campfire smoke, which genuinely is a
  passing nuisance. But campfire smoke disperses and irritates; welding fume deposits
  metal particles that the lungs cannot clear, and the dose adds up across thousands of
  arcs. Contrast the two: the cough ends in minutes, while the accumulated exposure can
  end in chronic lung and nervous-system disease decades later.</p>

  <h2>Fire: the hazard that waits</h2>
  <p>
    Sparks and slag — the brittle crust that forms on a weld — travel far further than the
    weld itself: through floor cracks, into rags and dust, along conduits. Lodged
    somewhere flammable, a spark can smoulder for an hour before flaring. The standard
    response is a clearing radius of about 11 metres (35 feet) around the work, fire
    blankets where combustibles cannot be moved, an extinguisher within reach, and a
    <strong>fire watch</strong> — a person whose only job is to look for ignition — who
    stays at least 30 minutes after the last arc.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the fire watch is there to put out fires <em>during</em>
  welding. This feels right because the watch stands beside the welder while sparks fly.
  But sparks rarely ignite on impact — they smoulder. The watch's critical job is the half
  hour <em>after</em> the work stops, catching the delayed ignition nobody saw begin.</p>

  <h2>Electricity: the invisible circuit through you</h2>
  <p>
    A welding circuit runs tens to hundreds of amps at voltages around 60–100 volts
    open-circuit — enough to push a dangerous current through damp skin. Shock happens when
    the body bridges the electrode side of the circuit to the work: wet gloves, damaged
    lead insulation, coiled cables that heat up, or changing an electrode with bare skin
    pressed against the workpiece. The defences are procedural and cheap: dry insulated
    gauntlet gloves, a daily inspection of leads and connections, a well-clamped work lead,
    dry footing, and never standing in water while welding.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="damp gloves and a scuffed lead — decide" widget="branch" config={wldShockBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> four mechanisms — radiation that burns unseen, particles that
    accumulate, sparks that smoulder, current that takes your body as a conductor — and
    against each a matching system: filters and screens, extraction, clearing and watching,
    inspection and dry insulation. Now we look at the gear and the shop that carry those
    systems.
  </p>

  <h2>PPE: engineered answers to specific hazards</h2>
  <p>
    Every item of welding PPE exists because one of the four mechanisms demands it, and the
    engineering shows. The <strong>helmet</strong> carries filter plates graded by shade;
    auto-darkening versions switch from clear to dark in tens of milliseconds, but their
    settings still have to be checked — a helmet that darkens late or light is worse than
    none, because it invites you to trust it. <strong>Leathers</strong> — jacket or sleeves,
    gauntlet gloves, spats over boot laces — block ultraviolet and shed spatter. Fabric
    rules follow the physics: cotton chars and lets spatter burn in, synthetics melt into
    skin, so the dress code is flame-resistant fabric with no cuffs or pockets where sparks
    can lodge.
  </p>
  <p>
    Eye protection continues between arcs and after them: clear safety glasses under the
    hood against flying spatter, and a full faceshield for grinding — in many shops' injury
    logs, grinders hurt more welders than arcs do. Fume-rated respirators, where the
    ventilation needs backup, carry particulate filters sized for the contaminant; supplied
    air, not filters, is required in confined spaces.
  </p>

  <h2>Ventilation: the control that outranks the respirator</h2>
  <p>
    The hierarchy put engineering first, and fume is where it pays. <strong>Local exhaust
    ventilation</strong> (LEV) is a fume-extraction arm or nozzle positioned within about
    30 cm of the arc, pulling the plume away before it crosses the breathing zone — capture
    works by proximity, which is why an arm parked half a metre away can run noisily and
    still capture almost nothing. General dilution — fans and open doors — is the weaker
    second choice, because it thins fume rather than removing it. Respirators come last,
    as the layer for what extraction cannot catch.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="one line of this fume plan is wrong" widget="spot-error" config={wldFumeSpot} />
  <p>
    Two situations override every ordinary rule. <strong>Confined spaces</strong> — tanks,
    pipes, ship compartments — trap fume and can also lose oxygen to shielding gases like
    argon, which displaces air without smell or struggle; work there requires extraction,
    gas monitoring and a permit system before anyone enters. And <strong>coated or plated
    metals</strong> — galvanised, painted, stainless — change the fume's chemistry, so the
    extraction and filter choices change with them.
  </p>

  <h2>Compressed gas cylinders: stored energy in steel</h2>
  <p>
    The gases that shield and fuel arcs — argon, carbon dioxide, acetylene, oxygen — arrive
    in cylinders holding gas at pressures of hundreds of times atmospheric, which makes
    cylinder handling a safety subject of its own. The rules are physical, not bureaucratic.
    Cylinders stand <strong>upright and chained</strong>, because a falling cylinder can
    shear its own valve and turn 50 kilograms of steel into a projectile; valve caps go on
    whenever a cylinder moves or waits; cylinders ride proper carts, never rolled or
    dragged.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Oxygen earns special respect</strong> for a reason worth understanding: oxygen
    is not flammable, but it is the ingredient every fire needs, and in concentrated form
    it makes ordinary materials burn violently. Oil or grease on an oxygen fitting can
    ignite spontaneously under the pressure and flow — so oxygen equipment never touches
    hydrocarbons, and fuel-gas cylinders are stored apart from oxygen. <strong>Acetylene</strong>
    adds its own chemistry: the gas is unstable above roughly 15 psi of pressure, so its
    regulators are set low, and the cylinders are used upright because acetylene is
    dissolved in acetone within a porous filler — tipped over, the solvent can leak out
    with the gas. Regulators get inspected for creeping gauges, hoses for cracks, and
    flashback arrestors — valves that stop a flame travelling back into the hose — fit on
    fuel-gas lines. The module compresses to one sentence: treat every cylinder as a rocket
    with a valve.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="true or false — cylinders and their chemistry" widget="judge" config={wldGasJudge} />

  <h2>Shop and site practice: the systems that hold</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>hot-work permit</strong> is where the hierarchy's administrative layer
    becomes visible. Before arc or flame, a formal authorisation records that combustibles
    were cleared, equipment checked, an extinguisher staged, a fire watch posted and a
    responsible person's sign-off given. The permit exists because the fires that happen
    are almost always the fires nobody watched, and because writing the checks down turns
    good intentions into an auditable system — most industrial jurisdictions require it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Around the permit sits the daily routine of a safe shop: leads inspected and uncoiled,
    cylinders chained upright with caps on when moved, floors clear of oil and offcuts,
    workpieces grounded through the work lead rather than through building structures,
    screens up so everyone in sight of the arc is protected. None of it is glamorous; all
    of it is the reason most welders finish their careers uninjured.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> safety systems are paperwork that slows real work down.
  The permit, the fire watch and the daily inspection look bureaucratic next to the
  immediacy of striking an arc. But each procedure encodes a specific failure that already
  happened to someone — smouldering fires, sheared valves, electrocutions — and the paper
  trail is what makes those failures repeatable to prevent. The welder who skips the
  checks is not saving time; they are borrowing it at the worst possible interest.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why does arc eye develop hours after the exposure, not during it?</li>
    <li>Rank these three defences against fume from most to least reliable, and say why: a respirator, a fume-extraction arm at the arc, an open door.</li>
    <li>A fire watch has stood beside the welder all morning with no fire. The arc stops at noon. When can the watch leave, and why?</li>
    <li>Why are oxygen cylinders never stored beside fuel-gas cylinders, and why must oxygen fittings stay free of oil?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The damage is done by ultraviolet radiation, which the
    eye cannot sense while absorbing it — there is no heat warning, so the inflammation
    only announces itself hours later. (2) Extraction arm first (engineering — removes the
    fume before anyone breathes it, works even on a forgetful day), respirator second (PPE
    — protects one wearer, fails silently if worn badly), open door last (dilution — thins
    the fume but does not remove it, and depends on the wind). (3) No sooner than 30
    minutes after the last arc — sparks that lodged in cracks or dust smoulder before
    flaring, and the watch exists for exactly that delayed ignition. (4) Oxygen is not
    flammable but is what every fire consumes; concentrated oxygen turns ordinary
    materials violently combustible, so a fuel-gas leak beside it — or grease ignited by
    pressurised oxygen on a fitting — removes the margin between nuisance and disaster.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Welding's four hazard families — arc radiation, fume, fire, electricity — all deliver damage that is delayed or invisible; defences are built around that.</li>
    <li>The hierarchy of controls orders defences: engineer the hazard away, then procedure, then PPE.</li>
    <li>Arc radiation burns eye and skin without warmth; shade-numbered filters and screens protect welder and bystander alike.</li>
    <li>Fume is a dose-accumulating aerosol of metal oxides; capture it at the source with local exhaust, not by dilution.</li>
    <li>Sparks smoulder: clear 11 m (35 ft), blanket what cannot move, keep a fire watch 30 minutes after the arc stops.</li>
    <li>Shock comes from bridging electrode to work: dry gloves, daily lead inspection, dry footing.</li>
    <li>Cylinders are stored energy: upright, chained, capped; oxygen away from oil and fuel gas; acetylene low-pressure and upright.</li>
    <li>Hot-work permits turn good intentions into an auditable system.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Safety first is not just a slogan — it is the licence for everything that follows. The
    next lessons put the arc to work: stick welding (SMAW), where you will meet the machine,
    polarity and electrode selection that were only names here, and then the wire and TIG
    processes. Every one of those lessons assumes the habits built on this page — helmet
    shade checked, extraction positioned, leads inspected, fire watch considered — running
    automatically before the first arc. That is what "safety first" means in practice: not
    a separate subject, but the operating system the rest of the trade runs on.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Shade numbers scale with amperage and process.</strong> A filter's job is to cut
    the arc's radiant output to a safe dose at the retina, so the requirement rises with
    current: light gas welding at low shades, stick and MIG in the 10–13 band at everyday
    amperages, heavy high-amperage work pushing toward shade 14. Standards such as ANSI
    Z49.1 and the EN 169/379 families publish the tables, and auto-darkening filters are
    rated by shade range and switching speed — reading those ratings is part of selecting a
    helmet, not just wearing one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Exposure limits give fume control its numbers.</strong> Occupational hygienists
    set permissible exposure limits and stricter threshold limit values per substance, in
    milligrams per cubic metre over an eight-hour shift; manganese, hexavalent chromium and
    nickel carry especially low limits. Ventilation engineering then works backwards from
    those numbers — capture velocity at the arc, duct sizing, filter stages, and air
    monitoring with personal sampling pumps on representative days. Fume control is a
    measurable engineering problem, not a matter of tolerating a smoky shop.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Electrical safety has its own quantitative layer.</strong> Open-circuit
    voltages of 60–100 volts sit above the thresholds where damp skin conducts dangerous
    current, and the classic risk factors — perspiration, conductive workpieces, body
    contact with the electrode circuit — are well documented in accident investigations.
    Modern machines add voltage-reduction devices that drop the open-circuit voltage when
    no arc is struck, and regulations distinguish the electrode side (live during arcing)
    from the work side. The old rule "never change rods with bare skin on the work"
    survives every generation of equipment because the mechanism does not change.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Site work multiplies every hazard.</strong> Confined spaces add oxygen
    displacement and permit systems; heights add fall protection to hot-work permits;
    weather adds wind that strips shielding gas and rain that adds shock risk. The
    underlying method stays constant — identify the hazard, engineer the control, verify,
    document — which is why safety, taught first, is also the skill that transfers furthest
    across a welding career.
  </p>

